How You Know Aria Is Correct
Layer 1: Deterministic Allocation Engine (the big one)
The single most important design decision is that the LLM does not choose percentages. The allocation is computed by pure, testable Python in allocation_engine.py:359-392:
risk band + goal + horizon
  → 7-bucket base (real US return/vol data)
  → goal tilt → horizon glide → floor → normalize
  → rollup to 3 classes (Equities/Debt/Commodities)
  → Commodities clamp ~10% → normalize to exactly 100
Verification: The exact numbers Aria speaks are the same numbers pushed to the Allocation tab via the computeAllocation WebSocket broadcast. There's no double-hop where the LLM invents numbers and the frontend re-parses them from the transcript. The old version had this bug (duplicate "Commodities" / 110% allocations) — the engine eliminates it entirely.
Ground truth data: The engine loads data/asset_stats.json which cites Damodaran/NYU Stern, Ibbotson/SBBI, and FTSE Nareit as sources (multi-decade US nominal returns):
- US Large Cap: 10.0% return / 15.5% volatility
- US Small Cap: 11.8% / 20.0%
- Gold: 7.7% / 15.0%
- Cash: 3.3% / 3.1%
Layer 2: Unit Tests (test_allocation_engine.py)
15 tests covering the allocation engine + feasibility math:
Test	What it proves
test_split_sums_100	7-bucket split always sums to ~100 for all risk bands
test_three_classes_sum_100	3-class rollup always equals exactly 100
test_risk_ordering	Aggressive has more equities than conservative
test_commodities_pinned	Commodities always lands at 9-11%
test_bands_match_aria_ranges	Output falls inside Aria's published prompt ranges
test_avoid_class	Avoiding a class zeros it + still sums to 100
test_interest_tilt	"More equities" actually raises equities
